WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.960
2017 was a brutal year of celeb losses.

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:08.240
From beloved musicians to fan-favored actors,
the year claimed a lot of high-profile lives.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.820
Here are some of the biggest celebs we lost
last year.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.709
Chris Cornell

00:00:13.709 --> 00:00:17.850
Soundgarden and Audioslave frontman Chris
Cornell's career spanned three decades.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:22.529
The rocker founded Soundgarden in 1984, and
the band became one of the most popular and

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:27.140
influential groups in the burgeoning Seattle
scene that would come to define grunge.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:31.619
Soundgarden reached the mainstream with their
1994 album Superunknown, which featured the

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:34.470
Grammy-winning singles "Black Hole Sun" and
"Spoonman."

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:39.250
And after the band broke up in 1997, Cornell
remained in the spotlight with a string of

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:44.130
solo albums and as singer of the supergroup
Audioslave before Soundgarden reunited in

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.620
2010.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:50.380
Cornell also contributed to several movie
soundtracks including those for Singles, Mission:

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.840
Impossible 2, and 12 Years a Slave.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:58.490
He received a Grammy nomination for his Casino
Royale theme "You Know My Name" and an Emmy

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:02.170
nomination for "The Keeper" from 2011's Machine
Gun Preacher.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:06.450
Unfortunately, Cornell ended his life by hanging
on May 18th.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:11.140
The 52-year-old singer was found in his hotel
room in Detroit, shortly after his final concert

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.250
with Soundgarden ended.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:14.750
Sam Shepard

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:19.260
Decorated playwright and actor Sam Shepard
was the very definition of a double-threat.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:25.760
The winner of 13 Obie Awards, he won his first
six for plays he wrote between 1966 and 1968.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.000
He soon made the transition into screenwriting
with films like Me and My Brother.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Then, in 1978, he stepped in front of the
camera as a land baron in Terrence Malick's

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.110
Days of Heaven,

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.190
He continued to work as a playwright, and
penned his critically acclaimed Family Trilogy

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:45.210
-- the first installment of which would earn
him the 1979 Pulitzer Prize for Drama.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:50.110
But he also thrived as a character actor at
the same time, earning an Oscar nomination

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:52.450
for playing Chuck Yeager in The Right Stuff.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:57.680
In his later years, Shepard appeared in movies
like Black Hawk Down, The Notebook, The Assassination

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.250
of Jesse James by the Coward Robert Ford,
Midnight Special, and the Netflix series Bloodline.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:09.390
Shepard ultimately lost his battle with ALS
on July 27th at the age of 73.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:10.910
Jerry Lewis

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:15.410
Comedy legend Jerry Lewis was one of the most
recognizable and influential American entertainers

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:16.970
of the 20th century.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:21.280
The actor skyrocketed to fame in the '50s
when he teamed with crooner Dean Martin as

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.420
the comedy duo Martin and Lewis.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.440
At the height of their popularity, they dominated
nightclubs, radio, and the box office.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.560
After a bitter split with Martin, Lewis remained
a top-grossing movie star through the mid-'60s

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.840
with classics like The Bellboy and The Nutty
Professor.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.170
His screen work continued all the way until
2016.

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:44.600
Lewis also became well-known as the national
chairman of the Muscular Dystrophy Association,

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.830
hosting the annual Labor Day telethon for
more than five decades, raising almost $2.5

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.890
billion over the course of his extended tenure.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.190
He was nominated for a Nobel Peace Prize in
1977.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:02.670
Sadly, Lewis passed away on August 20th at
the age of 91 after succumbing to heart failure.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:04.810
Harry Dean Stanton

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:09.560
Prolific character actor Harry Dean Stanton
enjoyed his first major role in the 1958 Western

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.140
The Proud Rebel.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:16.480
He also turned in a memorable performance
as a singing convict in the 1967 classic Cool

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.480
Hand Luke.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:22.210
He was a regular presence on TV for decades,
and he gained notoriety for roles in Two-Lane

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:28.030
Blacktop, Alien, Escape From New York, and
Repo Man, landing a rare leading role in Paris,

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.030
Texas.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:33.110
He later appeared in Pretty in Pink, The Last
Temptation of Christ, The Green Mile, and

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:35.959
had a cameo in Marvel's The Avengers.

00:03:35.959 --> 00:03:40.080
Stanton often collaborated with David Lynch,
appearing in Wild at Heart, Twin Peaks, The

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.860
Straight Story, and more.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.430
Despite his prolific career, he remained humble
'til the very end.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.790
"Anybody can be an actor, I feel.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:48.790
Anybody here.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:54.599
Everybody in this room, everywhere can be
a film actor, if you've got a good director."

00:03:54.599 --> 00:03:58.849
The curtain closed on the actor's life and
career on September 15th in Los Angeles.

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:00.560
He was 91.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:02.040
Chuck Low

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:07.330
Known for his part in Martin Scorsese's Goodfellas,
actor Chuck Low spent four years on active

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:11.890
duty as an army major and remained in the
reserves for three decades.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:15.650
His friendship with Robert De Niro opened
the door for Low's first onscreen appearance

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:17.810
in 1982's The King of Comedy.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:23.500
He also acted in other De Niro movies, including
Once Upon a Time in America and The Mission,

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:27.620
but he's still best remembered for playing
flamboyant wig shop owner "Morrie" Kessler

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.000
in Goodfellas.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.730
He later guest starred in small screen series
like The Sopranos and 100 Centre Street.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:37.900
Low's life came to an end on September 18th
at a nursing home in New Jersey.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.610
He was 89.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:40.910
Monty Hall

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:45.090
Co-creator and host of the game show Let's
Make a Deal, Monty Hall took leave of the

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:49.090
world on September 30th due to heart failure,
at the age of 96.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:54.100
A native of Canada, his career started in
radio commentary, but he became a television

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.210
legend when he created his hit game show series
in 1963.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:02.160
The show featured contestants who would come
to the studio dressed in crazy costumes in

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.880
an attempt to get Hall's attention.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:08.970
Then they could pick from a hidden prize or
take cash rolled up in Hall's jacket.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:13.600
Hall would often go off script and engage
in hilarious conversations with the players.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.910
The series eventually moved to ABC, running
in daytime, and occasionally in primetime,

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:21.010
for decades, and later ran in syndication.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:26.200
In all, Hall estimated he hosted more than
4,700 episodes.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.710
Tom Petty

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:32.150
American music history wouldn't be the same
without Tom Petty and the Heartbreakers.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:36.381
The band rose to fame in the mid-'70s and
was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of

00:05:36.381 --> 00:05:38.110
Fame in 2002.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:42.690
Petty was also a member of the '80s supergroup
the Traveling Wilburys with Bob Dylan, George

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.920
Harrison, Roy Orbison, and Jeff Lynne.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:50.389
And he sold more than 80 million records worldwide,
making him one of the top-selling musicians

00:05:50.389 --> 00:05:51.389
of all time.

00:05:51.389 --> 00:05:55.810
He also acted, making cameos in films like
Made in Heaven and opposite Kevin Costner

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.100
in The Postman.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.220
He also had a recurring voice role as Lucky
on King of the Hill.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:08.320
After spending decades in the spotlight, Petty's
story came to an end on October 2nd.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:12.960
The 66-year-old was found unconscious the
night before and was rushed to the hospital.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.910
After his family learned he had no brain activity,
they made the decision to withdraw him from

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.680
life support.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:19.680
Ralphie May

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.650
A comedian who rose to fame after appearing
during the first season of Last Comic Standing,

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:28.870
Ralphie May had been working his way to the
top for years before the world knew his name.

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:33.510
He'd previously won a contest to open for
Sam Kinison when he was 17, which prompted

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:36.320
him to pursue comedy as a career.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.350
After working the stand-up circuit for years,
he eventually released a series of comedy

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:42.990
specials and dabbled in scripted acting.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:48.090
Tragically, May passed on October 6th from
cardiac arrest after battling pneumonia for

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:49.860
several weeks.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.830
Roy Dotrice

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:57.770
After portraying a Pyromancer on Game of Thrones,
actor Roy Dotrice left this world on October

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:00.389
16th at the age of 94.

00:07:00.389 --> 00:07:05.520
Dotrice served in the British Royal Air Force
during WWII and was imprisoned in a German

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:06.520
POW camp.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:12.030
He began working as an actor in the '60s and
appeared in films like Amadeus and Hellboy 2.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:17.790
On television, he appeared on series like
Space 1999 and Beauty and the Beast.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:23.310
In 2000, he won a Tony Award for his role
in the Broadway revival of A Moon for the

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:24.320
Misbegotten.

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:28.550
However, Dotrice is perhaps best known for
Game of Thrones.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:33.180
He was reportedly offered the larger role
of Maester Pycelle but had to back out due

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:34.570
to health issues.

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:38.470
Instead, he played the head of the Alchemists'
Guild in King's Landing.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:43.910
But before that, Dotrice provided voices for
224 characters in the audiobook of the first

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:50.120
novel in George R.R. Martin's series A Song
of Ice and Fire, which inspired Game of Thrones.

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:55.199
He holds the Guinness World Record for playing
the most characters in an audiobook performance.

00:07:55.199 --> 00:07:56.789
Brad Bufanda

00:07:56.789 --> 00:08:02.350
Best known for playing Felix Toombs on Veronica
Mars, Brad Bufanda was also a small screen

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:07.500
staple in shows like CSI: Miami, Malcolm in
the Middle, Even Stevens, Boston Public, and

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:08.750
Days of Our Lives.

00:08:08.750 --> 00:08:13.590
The actor perished on November 1st at the
age of 34 after reportedly jumping from a

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:15.160
building in Los Angeles.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:19.400
A note was found on or near his body with
the names of his parents and a "thank you"

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.780
to people in his life.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:22.780
Della Reese

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:28.250
Best known for her work in the CBS drama series
Touched by an Angel, Della Reese started singing

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:32.949
at an early age and formed her own gospel
group, signing her first record contract in

00:08:32.949 --> 00:08:36.349
the '50s and topping the charts with "Don't
You Know."

00:08:36.349 --> 00:08:42.409
In the late '60s, she began acting on TV before
launching her own talk show in 1969.

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:46.189
She became the first African-American woman
to guest-host The Tonight Show with Johnny

00:08:46.189 --> 00:08:52.649
Carson in 1970 and later appeared on TV series
including Chico and the Man and The Love Boat.

00:08:52.649 --> 00:08:58.550
In 1979, Reese suffered a near-fatal aneurysm
that required two surgeries, but she bounced

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:01.570
back and was later ordained as a minister.

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:07.230
She became a household name playing Roma Downey's
sarcastic angel boss Tess on Touched by an

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.769
Angel, which aired from 1994 to 2003.

00:09:10.769 --> 00:09:15.129
Reese earned two Emmy nominations for the
role and reprised it in the spin-off, Promised

00:09:15.129 --> 00:09:16.300
Land.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:21.050
The actress passed away on November 19th at
the age of 86.

00:09:21.050 --> 00:09:22.519
David Cassidy

00:09:22.519 --> 00:09:27.130
Thanks to his role in the popular musical
sitcom The Partridge Family, David Cassidy

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.350
was also a legend of the small screen.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:33.490
He grew up as part of a show business family
and signed on to play Keith Partridge on The

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:37.819
Partridge Family in 1970 alongside his stepmother
Shirley Jones.

00:09:37.819 --> 00:09:42.779
The pair were the only cast members on the
show who were actually allowed to sing, and

00:09:42.779 --> 00:09:47.529
Cassidy performed the theme song "C'mon Get
Happy," which helped launch his music career.

00:09:47.529 --> 00:09:52.320
With hit singles including "I Think I Love
You" and "Cherish," Cassidy became a multimedia

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:53.740
superstar.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:57.629
He went on to make guest appearances on Fantasy
Island and The Love Boat.

00:09:57.629 --> 00:10:02.730
Cassidy also performed in the musical theater
scene and headlined several Las Vegas shows.

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:08.490
His daughter, Katie Cassidy, is also an actress,
playing Laurel Lance in the CW's Arrow.

00:10:08.490 --> 00:10:13.829
He passed away from organ failure on November
21st at the age of 67.

00:10:13.829 --> 00:10:14.949
Rose Marie

00:10:14.949 --> 00:10:19.850
Best known for playing Sally Rogers on The
Dick Van Dyke Show, Rose Marie's family announced

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.209
her passing on December 28th.

00:10:22.209 --> 00:10:27.339
The actress began her career as a child star
under the name Baby Rose Marie and starred

00:10:27.339 --> 00:10:30.889
on her own NBC radio show at the age of 5.

00:10:30.889 --> 00:10:36.239
She went on to appear in vaudeville, and had
parts in the 1929 short Baby Rose Marie the

00:10:36.239 --> 00:10:41.179
Child Wonder and the 1933 feature International
House.

00:10:41.179 --> 00:10:45.639
As a teenager, Marie became a nightclub singer
before returning to radio.

00:10:45.639 --> 00:10:51.399
In the early '50s, she appeared on television
variety shows, and in 1961, she was cast as

00:10:51.399 --> 00:10:56.720
Sally Rogers, a comedy television writer on
The Dick Van Dyke Show, a role which would

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.600
earn her three Emmy nominations.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:04.300
She was later a fixture on game shows, serving
as the upper center square on Hollywood Squares

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:08.179
for 14 years, and she also worked the nightclub
circuit and continued to make appearances

00:11:08.179 --> 00:11:12.480
on sitcoms like Murphy Brown, Wings, and Caroline
in the City.

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:15.929
She was 94 when she said finally goodbye to
the world.

00:11:15.929 --> 00:11:17.290
Thanks for watching!

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:20.850
For our tribute to the other celebrities who
passed away during the first half of the year,

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:25.489
please check out our other video, "Stars We've
Sadly Lost So Far In 2017."

